(Plan 310 task 5 - the manifest gives a thing a callable API, and
 `To do the point's <name>` defines one of the members it declares. Two call
 forms reach a member: the type possessive `a point's 'placed at' with 1 and
 0`, which is the only way to call a maker at all, and - when the member
 takes its own thing first - task 4's instance possessive as well.

 Every declared member returns its owner (§4). That is what makes the
 manifest mean "the functions that produce or transform this thing"; a
 function computing something else from a point, like the 'magnitude
 squared' of tests/336_instance_sugar.vox, stays a global function and needs
 no manifest entry.

 'grid square' declares 'placed at' too. The two definitions compile under
 distinct internal names, so a member name belongs to its owner rather than
 to the program, and this is also the only place the type possessive is read
 from a quoted thing name.

 The tail proves `do` stays an ordinary identifier: `To do.` defines a
 function called do and `do.` calls it. Only `do` followed by `the
 <thing>'s` opens a member definition.

 Acceptance: the six lines in the .expected, in that order - the maker, the
 instance possessive, the same member reached by the type possessive with
 the receiver written out, both fields of the other thing's maker, and the
 ordinary function called do.)

A thing called point has
  a function called 'placed at',
  a function called 'reflected through the origin',
  a number called x is 0,
  a number called y is 0.

A thing called 'grid square' has
  a function called 'placed at',
  a number called column is 0,
  a number called row is 0.

To do the point's 'placed at', with a number called x and a number called y.
  a point called plotted.
  Set plotted's x to x.
  Set plotted's y to y.
  Return a point, plotted.

To do the point's 'reflected through the origin', with a point called original.
  a point called reflection.
  Set reflection's x to 0 subtract original's x.
  Set reflection's y to 0 subtract original's y.
  Return a point, reflection.

To do the 'grid square''s 'placed at', with a number called column and a number called row.
  a 'grid square' called square.
  Set square's column to column.
  Set square's row to row.
  Return a 'grid square', square.

The pin is a point's 'placed at' with 1 and 0.
Print pin's x.

The opposite is pin's 'reflected through the origin'.
Print opposite's x.

The 'opposite of the opposite' is a point's 'reflected through the origin' of opposite.
Print 'opposite of the opposite''s x.

The 'marked square' is a 'grid square''s 'placed at' with 5 and 6.
Print 'marked square''s column.
Print 'marked square''s row.

To do.
  Print "do is an ordinary name".

do.
